  "comboAudit": "Anchor set: every anchor is a standard Finnish aapinen-style keyword, sound-initial AND spelling-initial except the two documented medial vowels (y — kynä, ö — pöllö: no illustrated y-/ö-initial noun exists in the library; the vowel is heard stressed in the first syllable, following the 'ö niin kuin pöllö' classroom tradition). Clean-vowel-value convention enforced: a = apina (auto REJECTED — au is a diphthong that glides the vowel), e = etana, i = ikkuna, o = ovi, u = uuni (long uu ACCEPTED — identical vowel quality, only kesto differs; length is itself the Finnish lesson, unlike a diphthong which changes quality). m = muki chosen over muna as the tap-anchor (muna stays a speakable extraWord — standard aapinen vocabulary — but a repeat-tap anchor should not be a playground giggle magnet). All noun/themeDir pairs verified against the shipped fi Sound Boxes bank + Blending Board decks + activity manifests; gorilla/zoo animals additionally curl-verified live (HTTP 200 at image-library-webp/themes/zoo animals/gorilla@2x.webp, 2026-07-16). Guest letters: b (banaani) and g (gorilla) and d (delfiini) anchored — established, illustrated loanwords a Finnish child meets in print; c/f/w/x/z/å quiet (no clean illustrated Finnish anchor; å appears only in Swedish names). extraWords: ~298 high-frequency 1.-2. luokka words (function words, family, animals, nature, home, school, food, body, clothes, base verbs + common 3rd-person forms, colors, numbers 0-100, time), hand-swept for profanity/anatomy/alcohol — clean; no alcohol words (kalja/viina excluded), no toilet words. neverSpeak whitelists the buildable vulgar/anatomy/taunt class (kikkeli/pillu/paska/kusi/kakka class + perkele-class swears + tyhmä/idiootti taunts) so TTS can never voice them even if a child builds one. nonwordTTS true: Finnish TTS reads any letter string syllabically and correctly by the 1:1 äänne-kirjain rule — robot words are pedagogically useful, matching the shipped Blending Board fi decks.",
  "notes": "fi tray = a-z (minus q) + å ä ö, 28 tiles, NO digraph tiles — Finnish is strictly 1:1 äänne-kirjain; long sounds (kaksoiskirjaimet: kukka, kuu) are BUILT from two single tiles, which is the core spelling lesson of alkuopetus. y is a VOWEL (front rounded /y/), never a consonant. ä and ö are independent vowels, not variants of a/o. å is a quiet guest vowel (used only in Swedish loans/names — kept so the full Finnish alphabet row is present, as on classroom wall charts). q omitted entirely: not in the platform guest set and no Finnish child word uses it. Vowel harmony is NOT enforced on the open board — it is a word-level rule and the teacher's teaching point, not a tile constraint."
}
